From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Send modifiers to the client under the pointer

wl_pointer events carry no modifier state, and Mutter sent
wl_keyboard.modifiers only to the client with keyboard focus. A window
that is hovered but not focused therefore saw no Control or Shift on its
scroll, button, motion and gesture events: Ctrl+scroll did not zoom and
Shift+scroll did not pan until the window was clicked. X11, macOS and
Windows all report the modifiers with pointer events to the window under
the pointer without changing focus.

wayland.xml allows wl_keyboard.modifiers "without a surface of the client
having keyboard focus, for example to tie modifier information to pointer
focus instead", and a reset by sending no pressed modifiers. KWin has done
this since Plasma 6. GTK 3 and 4, Chromium and Electron, Qt 6 and
Xwayland already apply such an event to their pointer events.

Use that permission narrowly. The client that owns the pointer focus
surface, while it has no keyboard focus, is sent the current state right
before a pointer event is delivered to it (enter, motion, button, axis,
touchpad gesture), and only when it differs from what that client was last
sent; key presses are never streamed to it. The state comes from the
event itself where the event carries it. When the pointer leaves, a client
that was sent pressed or latched modifiers is sent a reset. Keyboard focus
changes, a new wl_keyboard and keymap or layout changes make the next
pointer event resend the state. No other client learns anything.

The new wayland-keyboard test checks the order on the wire, that nothing
is streamed while the pointer is still, the reset on leave, and that a
client neither focused nor hovered receives no modifier events.
